<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chandrapur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chandrapur, historically Chanda, is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near the confluence of the Wardha and Erai rivers. It was the capital of the Gond kingdom of Chanda from the 15th or 16th century, and its stone walls and the Mahakali temple date from that period. The kingdom fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> around 1751. The district is today a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chandrapur.jpg" alt="Chandrapur" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ganesh Dhamodkar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chandrapur_skyline.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chandrapur, earlier called Chanda, the official name until 1964, is a city and municipal corporation in Chandrapur district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, at the confluence of the Erai and Zarpat rivers. By tradition the place was once called Lokapura, then Indpur, and finally Chandrapur. According to the founding legend, the Gond king Khandkya Ballal Sah, afflicted with tumours, found a healing spring on the dry bed of the Zarpat River. The Achaleshwar Temple was raised there after the deity appeared to him in a dream, and he later founded the fortified city.</p><p>The area lies on terrain rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and is called a geological museum for its variety of rocks, minerals and fossils. There are many Stone Age sites in the district, and signs of pottery suggest settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area passed through many hands. Between 322 BCE and 187 BCE it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunga-empire">Shunga Empire</a> until 78 BCE and to the Satavahana Empire from the 1st century BCE to the 2nd century CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakataka dynasty</a> ruled from the mid-3rd century CE to 550, followed by the Kalachuri dynasty in the 6th and 7th centuries, the Rashtrakuta dynasty between the 7th and 10th centuries and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukya dynasty</a> to the 12th century. The Seuna (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a>) dynasty of Devagiri held a kingdom including the area from about 850 to 1334.</p><p>The Gond kings built the city's walled fort, with strong walls 15 to 20 feet high and four main gates, and the Mahakali temple, built around the 16th century by Dhundya Ram Sah. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> took Chandrapur in 1751 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> period. When the last Bhonsle ruler, Raghuji Bhonsle III, died in 1853 without issue, it was annexed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> under the Doctrine of Lapse. In 1956 the district was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> state, and in May 1960 it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chandrapur had a population of about 518,000 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> is spoken by most people, with Gondi spoken by most of the Gonds. It is a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mining. The old city, with the fort and the Anchaleshwar Temple, where the Gond kings' samadhi stands, lies at the river confluence.</p><p>The Tadoba Andhari Tiger Reserve lies about 30 kilometres north of the city. The Chandrapur Super Thermal Power Station, whose foundation stone was laid in 1977, supplies a large share of the state's electricity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chandrapur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The capital moved to Ballarpur and then, in the 15th and 16th centuries under Khandkya Ballal Shah, to <b>Chanda</b>, where a walled city was laid out.</blockquote><a href="/state/chanda-kingdom">Chanda Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Raigarh was ruled by a Raj Gond dynasty whose traditions say they came from <b>Chanda</b> (Chandrapur).</blockquote><a href="/state/raigarh-state">Raigarh State</a></li></ul></section>
